Here's how to build that exact "issue → analyze → generate tests → execute → report" pipeline on your own machine, using the Claude API. There are three ways to do it, from least to most code — I'd pick the middle one.

Option A — Zero code: Claude Code CLI in headless mode

If you just want a script you can drop in a cron job or CI step, no Python/TS needed:

bash
npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code   # or your platform's installer
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key

claude -p "Read GitHub issue #2 in mizanurdreamer/MCalendar (use gh issue view), \
analyze the login/dashboard code it describes, generate a Playwright test file \
covering its acceptance criteria, execute the tests, and write the results to \
report.md." \
  --allowedTools "Read,Write,Edit,Bash,Glob,Grep,WebFetch" \
  --permission-mode acceptEdits \
  --output-format json > run.json

This is literally what's driving this conversation — same agent loop, same tools, just invoked as a subprocess instead of interactively.

Option B — Claude Agent SDK (recommended)

This is the library version of the CLI — you write a script, but the tool loop (read files, run bash, write files, decide what's next) is handled for you. It's the right layer for something you'll run repeatedly or wire into your own tooling.

bash
python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install claude-agent-sdk
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key
gh auth login   # needed if the agent will call `gh` to read the issue
python
# run_pipeline.py
import asyncio
from claude_agent_sdk import query, ClaudeAgentOptions, AssistantMessage, ResultMessage

PROMPT = """
1. Read GitHub issue #2 in mizanurdreamer/MCalendar (`gh issue view 2 --json title,body`).
2. Analyze the repo code the issue describes (login flow, dashboard routes).
3. Generate a Playwright test file under tests/e2e/ covering the issue's
   acceptance criteria.
4. Install dependencies and execute the tests.
5. Write a markdown report (REPORT.md) summarizing what was tested, the
   pass/fail result, and any caveats.
"""

async def main():
    options = ClaudeAgentOptions(
        cwd="/path/to/your/local/clone/MCalendar",
        allowed_tools=["Read", "Write", "Edit", "Bash", "Glob", "Grep", "WebFetch"],
        permission_mode="acceptEdits",  # auto-approve edits; still asks for risky Bash by default
        system_prompt=(
            "You are a QA automation engineer. Work end-to-end: read the "
            "story, write real tests, actually run them, and report the "
            "real result — never claim a test passed without executing it."
        ),
    )

    async for message in query(prompt=PROMPT, options=options):
        if isinstance(message, AssistantMessage):
            for block in message.content:
                if hasattr(block, "text"):
                    print(block.text)
                elif hasattr(block, "name"):
                    print(f"→ tool: {block.name}")
        elif isinstance(message, ResultMessage):
            print(f"Done: {message.subtype}")

asyncio.run(main())
bash
python run_pipeline.py

Key options worth knowing:

allowed_tools — whitelist exactly what the agent can touch (add WebSearch if it needs to look things up online).
permission_mode — "acceptEdits" auto-approves file edits but still prompts for shell commands by default; "bypassPermissions" auto-approves everything (only use this in a disposable sandbox/VM — a Bash-enabled agent with no guardrails can run destructive commands).
system_prompt — this is where you bake in "always actually execute the tests" so it doesn't just narrate.

TypeScript works the same way via npm install @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk and the query() function — same shape, async for/for await either way.

Option C — Raw Messages API (full control, most work)

If you're not using Python/TS, or want to own the loop yourself, use the API directly with the bash tool and implement the read-execute-respond cycle:

python
import anthropic

client = anthropic.Anthropic()  # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from env

response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5",
    max_tokens=4096,
    tools=[{"type": "bash_20250124", "name": "bash"}],
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": PROMPT}],
)
# Loop: while response.stop_reason == "tool_use", execute the requested
# command yourself, send back a tool_result, and call messages.create again.

This is what the Agent SDK is doing internally — you'd need to write the persistent bash session, the loop, retries, and context management yourself, which is why B is usually the better starting point unless you have a reason to avoid the SDK.

A few things that'll matter regardless of which option you pick

Run it in a disposable environment (container/VM), not your main machine — a Bash-enabled agent executes real shell commands. Scope allowed_tools to what the task needs. And put "actually execute, don't just describe" in your prompt/system prompt — it's the difference between a real report and a plausible-sounding one.